      "math_academy_id": "q-21072",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical",
      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal.",
      "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $"
            },
