      "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical.",
      "worked_solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical.",
